= Computer architecture =

== Overview ==
'''Computer architecture''' concerns the organization and programmer-visible structure of computer systems.

It includes instruction-set design, processors, memory systems, input/output, storage interfaces, parallelism, and system organization.

== Instruction set architecture ==
An instruction set architecture (ISA) defines the interface visible to machine-code software, such as:

* instructions;
* registers;
* data types;
* addressing modes;
* memory model;
* privilege mechanisms;
* exception behavior.

Different processors can implement the same ISA.

== Architecture and microarchitecture ==
'''Architecture''' describes externally significant structure and behavior.

'''Microarchitecture''' describes an implementation of that architecture, such as pipelines, caches, execution units, branch prediction, and internal scheduling.

Therefore:

<pre>
architecture ≠ microarchitecture
</pre>

== Memory hierarchy ==
Registers, caches, main memory, and storage have different latency, bandwidth, capacity, persistence, and cost.

Computer design exploits locality to bridge these gaps.

== Parallelism ==
Architectures can exploit instruction-level, data-level, thread-level, multicore, vector, GPU, or distributed forms of parallel execution.

Parallel hardware does not automatically make software parallel.

== Architecture and operating systems ==
[[Operating system|Operating systems]] manage and virtualize architectural resources.

The architecture provides mechanisms; the operating system establishes policies and abstractions over them.

